---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Eca from '../../components/Eca.astro';
import EcaTable from '../../components/EcaTable.astro';
import Deck from '../../components/Deck.astro';
import CaGrid from '../../components/CaGrid.astro';
import CaNet from '../../components/CaNet.astro';
---
<Base title="Cellular automata" description="Interactive cellular automata: any Wolfram rule 0–255 on a row of cells, with a walkthrough of rule 90 for a crowd that plays it with their fingers; then the parity rule on a grid, where a smiley copies itself, and on networks, where a single seed spreads from the best- or the worst-connected node." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Cellular automata</h1>
    <p>
      Cells, each either on or off. Time runs in ticks. At every tick each cell looks only at its
      neighbours and at itself, a fixed rule says what it becomes next, and every cell switches at the
      same moment. That is the whole recipe. This page starts with the simplest case, a row of cells,
      then puts the same idea on a grid and finally on a network, where a cell's neighbours are whoever
      it happens to be wired to.
    </p>

    <h2 id="row">A row of cells</h2>
    <p>
      Each cell looks at three things only: its own state and the states of its left and right
      neighbour. Three cells make 8 possible neighbourhoods, and a rule assigns an outcome to each, so
      there are 2<sup>8</sup> = 256 rules, numbered 0 to 255. In the picture below the top row is the
      starting state and every row underneath is the next tick: the image is a history, not a snapshot.
      The row is a ring, so the last cell counts the first as its right neighbour. Click the table under
      the controls to flip one outcome and see the rule number change.
    </p>
    <Deck id="deck" note="Fullscreen, two slides: the rule, then this demo. A clicker or the arrow keys move forward; Esc leaves.">
      <section data-slide aria-label="Rule 90" style="--lut-size: clamp(0.9rem, 1.9vw, 1.8rem)">
        <h2>Rule 90</h2>
        <EcaTable rule={90} fingers />
        <p>Look left, look right. Ignore your own hand.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Exactly one neighbour up: your finger goes up.</strong><br />
          Both up, or both down: your finger goes down.
        </p>
        <p class="meta">Pick your two neighbours first and keep them. On the edge, take the person in front of you; if you cannot see two people, take the presenter.</p>
      </section>
      <section data-slide data-adopt="#eca" aria-label="Live demo"></section>
    </Deck>
  </div>

  <Eca rule={90} width={31} />

  <div class="measure">
    <p class="meta">
      Keys once the demo has focus (click it, go fullscreen or start presenting): space plays and
      pauses, → or PageDown steps, ← or PageUp undoes a round, r resets, f toggles fullscreen. A
      presentation clicker sends PageDown and PageUp. The address bar follows the settings, so a
      set-up can be bookmarked.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rule-90">Rule 90</h2>
    <EcaTable rule={90} fingers />
    <p>
      Read the eight outcomes from case 111 down to case 000 and you get the binary
      number <code>01011010</code>, which is 90 in decimal. That is where every rule gets its number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now look at what the table actually says: the outcome is 1 exactly when the left and right
      neighbours differ. Your own state plays no part. In crowd terms: <strong>look left, look right,
      ignore your own hand. If exactly one of your two neighbours has a finger up, put yours up for
      the next round. If both or neither do, put it down.</strong> Mathematically the new state is
      left XOR right, the sum of the two neighbours modulo 2.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start from a single finger up in the middle and rule 90 draws the Sierpinski triangle: the
      pattern spreads one seat per round in both directions, and the inside keeps hollowing out into
      smaller copies of the whole. Row <em>t</em> is row <em>t</em> of Pascal's triangle with the odd
      entries marked, because adding modulo 2 is exactly what the rule does.
    </p>

    <h2 id="crowd">Running it with a seated crowd</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Everyone picks two neighbours and keeps them for the whole game: normally the person on the
        left and the person on the right. On the edge of a row, take the person in front of you instead.
        If you cannot see two people, take the presenter.</li>
      <li>One person in the middle of the room starts with a finger up, everyone else down.</li>
      <li>Each round: look at your two neighbours, not at your own hand, and decide. Nobody moves yet. On
        "three", everyone changes at once. Deciding and changing have to be separate steps, or the quick
        ones will contaminate the round for the slow ones.</li>
      <li>Press <em>Start presenting</em> at the top of this page. The first slide is the rule as the
        crowd should hear it; one press forward on the clicker brings up the demo. From then on forward
        steps a round and back undoes one. Back at round 0 returns to the rule slide, and Esc leaves.
        Set the number of people as cells on the demo slide itself.</li>
      <li>The demo shows the clean case, one straight row of that many cells. A real room, with
        neighbours picked across rows, drifts away from the picture after a few rounds. The rule is the
        same; the wiring is not, and that is worth saying out loud.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="on-a-grid">A grid of cells</h2>
    <p>
      Rule 90 has a name that travels: the <em>parity rule</em>. Count the neighbours with a finger up
      and go up if the count is odd, down if it is even. Said that way, the rule no longer cares how
      many neighbours there are or where they sit, so it works on anything. There are two versions.
      The <em>self-exclusive</em> one counts the neighbours only, which is rule 90 on a row.
      The <em>self-inclusive</em> one counts your own finger too, which is rule 150.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a grid you first have to say who counts as a neighbour. The <em>von Neumann</em> neighbourhood
      is the four cells you share an edge with. The <em>Moore</em> neighbourhood adds the four corners,
      making eight. The range-two von Neumann neighbourhood takes every cell at most two steps away
      along the grid lines, a diamond of twelve. The small pictogram in the corner of the picture shows
      the one in use. The grid wraps at the edges, like the row did, so it is really a doughnut.
    </p>
  </div>
  <CaGrid />
  <div class="measure">
    <p>
      The parity rule has a party trick. Whatever you start with, after 2, 4, 8, 16 ticks the pattern
      comes back as several exact copies of itself, one for every cell in the neighbourhood, each shifted
      that many cells along. With the four von Neumann neighbours a smiley becomes four smileys, then
      sixteen; include yourself and a fifth copy stays put in the middle. Edward Fredkin noticed this in
      the 1960s and the four-neighbour version has carried his name since. In between those ticks the
      picture is a mess, and that mess is what a crowd running rule 90 on a grid would see: every copy is
      built from Sierpinski triangles crossing one another. The trick works because the rule
      is <em>linear</em>: two patterns run at once give the sum of the two patterns run apart. Apply the
      rule twice and the cross terms cancel in pairs, so two ticks amount to one parity count over
      neighbours twice as far away; four ticks, four times as far; and a parity count over cells so far
      apart that they never overlap is nothing but a set of copies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things to try. Set the number of cells to 64 and watch the smiley vanish at tick 32: on a
      doughnut that many cells around, the copies meet their own mirror images and cancel. And switch on
      random: from a random start there is no picture to copy, and the rule looks like noise from then
      on, which it is not. It is still the same deterministic sum.
    </p>

    <h2 id="on-a-network">A network of cells</h2>
    <p>
      Now drop the grid. Each cell is a node in a network, its neighbours are the nodes it is linked to,
      and the rule is the same parity count. Three families of random network are on offer. A <em>small
      world</em> starts as a ring where every node knows its two neighbours on either side, then a
      fraction of the links are cut and reattached at random; at zero rewiring it is a ring, at full
      rewiring a tangle. A <em>random</em> network links every pair of nodes with the same probability,
      set here by the mean number of links. A <em>scale-free</em> network is grown one node at a time,
      each newcomer preferring to link to nodes that already have many links, which is how a few hubs and
      a great many leaves come about.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only one node starts up, and that is not a limitation. Because the rule is linear, any starting
      pattern evolves as the sum of its single seeds, so the single-seed runs are all there is to know.
      The slider picks which node it is, from the most to the least connected; the ring marks it. That is
      the simplest notion of a node's importance, the number of its links, and how much it explains is
      one of the questions in <a href="/research/">my current work</a>. Below the network the history is
      drawn the way the row was at the top of the page, time running down, one column per node.
    </p>
  </div>
  <CaNet />
  <div class="measure">
    <p>
      Things to watch. On the ring, the history is the picture from the top of the page again, only
      with two neighbours on each side: a triangle spreading two nodes per tick from the seed. Rewire a
      few links and shortcuts appear, the front jumps ahead somewhere far from the seed, and the triangle
      is gone within a handful of ticks. In a scale-free network the hub lights up all of its many
      neighbours in one tick; start from a leaf instead and the same thing happens a tick or two later,
      as soon as the activity reaches a hub. In the random network the least connected node may have no
      links at all, in which case nothing happens, and the readout says so. On most of these networks
      the run never comes to rest: roughly half the nodes are on at any tick, with no pattern the eye can
      catch. A linear rule, and yet as unpredictable to look at as anything on this page.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">
      Keys once a figure has focus: space plays and pauses, → or PageDown steps, ← or PageUp undoes a
      tick, r resets to the starting state, f toggles fullscreen. The address bar follows the settings
      of every figure, so a set-up can be linked.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
